# Voice Profile (example / starter template)

> Copy this file to `voice-profile.md` in the same directory and fill it in with
> your own voice. The copy is gitignored, so personal writing samples, employer
> details and self-description never enter the repository.
>
> This shared profile is the fallback for every agent that declares the
> `voice_profile` prompt include (`writer`, `content_reviewer`,
> `substack_publisher`, `substack_image_creator`). An agent-local
> `<agent>/prompts/voice-profile.md` overrides it for that one agent only.

## Sources & Precedence

List the texts this profile was derived from, and rank them. Then keep the rule
below, because it is the part that is easy to get wrong:

**Never re-derive a voice profile from LLM-assisted text.**

Assisted output is *approved* output — it passed your judgement — but it is not
evidence of how you write unaided. If you feed it back in, the profile learns the
assistant's habits (manufactured sentence-length variety, fragment runs, staccato
triplets) and presents them as yours. Every cycle then drifts further from you
while looking more like fidelity, because the profile and the recent samples agree
with each other.

Two practical consequences:

- Mark each source as unassisted / partly assisted / heavily assisted, and say
  which ones outrank the others.
- Check *within* a file too. Drafts often contain pasted assistant output
  alongside your own prose; a giveaway is the assistant addressing you
  ("Want me to add…?").

If a brand or style guide also exists, say which document wins for **voice** and
which wins for audience and positioning. They are usually not the same file: a
style guide describes the aspiration, a voice profile describes the habit.

## Core Persona

_Who is writing. Role, background, what they write from — experience, research,
practice. Two or three sentences._

## Register

_First language, formality, anything that reads as "error" but is actually voice.
Say explicitly what an editor must not smooth away._

## Tone

_e.g. "Direct, no hedging. Concedes the other position sincerely before stating
his own."_

## Rhythm

_Sentence length and paragraph shape, described from real samples. Avoid
prescribing variety quotas — those produce machine cadence, not voice._

## Humor & Provocation

_How they actually land, with quoted examples. This is the section most often
lost in editing, so it is the one worth being concrete about._

## Vocabulary & Punctuation

_Preferred words, banned words, contractions, em dash and bold budgets,
recurring idioms and self-references._

## Openings

_Ranked by what they actually do, not by what would be most striking._

## Closings

_Invitations, forward promises, honest uncertainty. What they never do._

## Signature Moves

_Devices observed in the samples — quote them. Reach for these before inventing
new ones._

## Structural Preferences

_Headers, lists, blockquotes, tables._

## The Test

_One question to ask of any sentence before shipping it._
